00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:00.520
And so it was decorated for us.

506
00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:06.200
In the original pilot, Brent's office was actually what became our recreation.

507
00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.900
And so they kind of switched those around.

508
00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:09.660
Yeah.

509
00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:13.840
So we were asked to keep the loft down.

510
00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:17.700
And I was like, is someone actually complaining about joy?

511
00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:18.600
Yes.

512
00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:20.980
Is someone actually complaining?

513
00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:25.820
If we were fighting and screaming and calling each other names, sure.

514
00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:26.140
Okay.

515
00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:26.680
Have a go.

516
00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:27.780
But this?

517
00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:28.140
No.

518
00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:29.660
What were they doing?

519
00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:30.800
What was the other business?

520
00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:34.180
I'm like, if you're really grumpy at your job and all you hear is people laughing, you

521
00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:34.880
might complain.

522
00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:36.280
You know, I don't know.

523
00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:37.180
That's a good question.

524
00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.000
I don't know.

525
00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:39.340
But was it Ricky's laugh?

526
00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:41.300
Because Ricky's laugh is really loud.

527
00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:42.220
It's loud.

528
00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:43.220
It's really, really loud.

529
00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:44.160
It's a cackle.

530
00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:45.580
They must know we were filming.

531
00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.880
I mean, like maybe it was part of the scene.

532
00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:48.680
Yes.

533
00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:49.820
It doesn't matter.

534
00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:50.980
They didn't like it.

535
00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:55.700
Did you film all the episodes at that same place?

536
00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:56.000
All of them.

537
00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:59.760
Except obviously anything on location, like the nightclub or...

538
00:23:59.760 --> 00:23:59.900
Sure.

539
00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:00.240
Right.

540
00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:00.520
Yeah.

541
00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:06.740
Well, I'm curious, like, you know, the first season aired and then became very popular and

542
00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:10.200
then you had to go and do the second one and you had to do the Christmas special.

543
00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.640
Did people figure out where you were filming?

544
00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:17.380
Did fans come or did this other business suddenly become enamored with you?

545
00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:18.160
Yeah.

546
00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:19.780
I don't think they complained in season two.

547
00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:25.060
I don't remember any press or any fans coming to the studio.

548
00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:25.940
That's good.

549
00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:27.020
You still had privacy.

550
00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:27.560
Yeah.

551
00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:34.740
We wanted to ask you about the front reception desk where you sit because we were asked by

552
00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:39.580
Greg and our director, Ken Kwapis, to personalize our desks, to bring things in from home.

553
00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:41.860
So it really felt like your workspace.

554
00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:42.440
Yeah.

555
00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:45.360
So we brought pictures and little tchotchke kind of things.

556
00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:45.700
Nice.

557
00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:51.180
There's a great shot when, you know, in the next scene, David Brent comes over to introduce

558
00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:51.700
you.

559
00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:51.960
Yeah.

560
00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.420
And there's a great shot of your desk.

561
00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:55.480
There's so many things.

562
00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:56.260
And I made a list of what you have.

563
00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:56.480
What's on it?

564
00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:57.900
I took a picture of it.

565
00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:00.680
You have a monkey calendar, a snowflake cup.

566
00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.180
You have two telephones.

567
00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:03.220
Two telephones.

568
00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:04.040
Oh, that's greedy.

569
00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:04.520
Mm-hmm.

570
00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:06.180
That wasn't nothing to do with me.

571
00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:10.480
I don't know why you have two telephones, but you do.

572
00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:13.880
So did you put anything on that desk or was it all there for you?

573
00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:18.600
Not in terms of, I didn't put personalize it in terms of what's seen on camera, but I

574
00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.080
had a ton of stuff that I bought in.

575
00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:23.480
I was a bit of a medicine desk for people.

576
00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:27.440
It was like, oh, have you got a, you know, paracetamol, which is like Tylenol.

577
00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:28.480
TuMs.

578
00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:29.340
TuMs.

579
00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:29.920
Yeah.

580
00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:31.560
I just had, I don't know why.

581
00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:32.280
I had so much stuff under there.

582
00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.640
Oh my gosh, you're the medicine lady.

583
00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:35.320
Angela is the sauce lady.

584
00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:36.420
Are you?

585
00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:42.760
If you need like a, she literally today brought in three little ketchup packets and a salt

586
00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:46.880
packet that she saves and then she puts them in a drawer for us.

587
00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:51.460
When you walked in and the first person you saw was Jenna and you guys hugged and I stood

588
00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:55.120
there holding my ketchup packets because she walked in right as I was putting them in the

589
00:25:55.120 --> 00:25:58.860
drawer and I was like wondering if you saw me holding ketchup packets.

590
00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.300
I didn't, but I do have a cupboard at home with things like that in.

591
00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:02.860
Oh.

592
00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:03.340
Yeah.

593
00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.580
Because I do love making a packed lunch.

594
00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.900
And you need, you need a portable sauce.

595
00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:12.560
I get very happy when I go, oh, I really need some, I've got it.

596
00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:12.900
Yeah.

597
00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:14.700
I've got a sachet of ketchup.

598
00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:19.760
Do you remember when like all of a sudden you couldn't get like the sriracha sauce?

599
00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:20.580
Remember?

600
00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.520
Like it was like in the stores.

601
00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:23.400
Anyone else, Sam?

602
00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:24.200
Sriracha sauce?

603
00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:25.360
I remember this, yes.

604
00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:26.380
You remember it.

605
00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:27.140
Thank you, Sam.

606
00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:28.620
I've never eaten sriracha sauce.

607
00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:34.000
Well, let me tell you who has sriracha packets and they're not easy to come by.

608
00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:34.660
I have a drawer.

609
00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:35.920
I have some sriracha packets.

610
00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:40.060
I kind of covet them in my sauce drawer.

611
00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:43.120
At least it's not an expensive habit you have.

612
00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:43.760
Thank you.

613
00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:44.040
Yeah.

614
00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:44.740
Thank you.

615
00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:48.040
Do you remember like what was the first scene you shot?

616
00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:52.920
Was it this scene where like did you kind of shoot in order like him coming up to your desk or no?

617
00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:54.680
What do you remember from that scene?

618
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:00.500
I'm pretty sure we did film it in order except for like, you know,

619
00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:06.560
so here's a scene at my desk and then there might be a scene in Brent's office and there might be a scene in the rec room.

620
00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:09.480
So we wouldn't necessarily have then gone in order.

621
00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:18.000
But we definitely went as in order as possible, which was why for me to play Dawn at the end where I get together with Tim,

622
00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:26.140
the nice thing about that is everything was played so much in order that it felt like a story that you were playing it out yourself.

623
00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:27.520
And that was actually pretty nice.

624
00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:28.780
That is really nice.

625
00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:29.440
I loved that.

626
00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:41.100
I was curious about your wardrobe, your costume for Dawn because I wore my own clothes in the pilot for ours and I didn't really have a business suit.

627
00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:44.580
I just kind of threw together what I had in a color palette of gray.

628
00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:49.400
But I didn't know, did you have any input in your look for the show?

629
00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:55.640
I mean, I don't think anyone came up and said, I think it was just like, you know, you've really got to just look office-y.

630
00:27:55.640 --> 00:28:01.200
And so that might pretty much be a suit, skirt and a shirt and it's Dawn.

631
00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:04.400
So it's not, I'm not like the boss, like Jennifer Taylor Clark.

632
00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:08.600
So it wouldn't be as smart or as expensive or as cute.

633
00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:09.140
You know what I mean?

634
00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.080
It was like soft colors.

635
00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:21.140
And so we used to, before the season started, I would go and meet with the wardrobe designer and we would go and walk around Oxford Street and look for, I hate shopping.

636
00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:22.080
Hate it.

637
00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:30.020
And so wardrobe was always my least favorite part of any job because now I have to go around the shops.

638
00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:34.200
So when I came here to America and I was like, they go to the shops for you?

639
00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:35.760
Oh, you went with?

640
00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:36.100
I was delighted.

641
00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:36.500
Yeah.

642
00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:38.580
Oh my goodness, I'm just putting that together.

643
00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:38.940
Yeah.

644
00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:40.200
That's so wild.

645
00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.960
Trying on 80 million things, spending the whole day.

646
00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:46.040
I'd be like, anything, I'll wear anything.

647
00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:47.740
You just tell me and I'll wear it.

648
00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:49.580
So is that standard?

649
00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:54.860
Like if you book a show in the UK, you go with the costume department?

650
00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:58.560
I can't remember a job I've done in the UK where I haven't.

651
00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.160
Yeah.

652
00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:00.100
Wow.

653
00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:00.560
Yeah.

654
00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:04.880
I did a show in the UK for Sky TV.

655
00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:05.520
Okay.

656
00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:08.020
But my character was in prison.

657
00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.420
Right, so that might be different.

658
00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:14.700
Most of the show in like a prison jumpsuit.

659
00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:15.180
Got it.

660
00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:15.620
Yeah.

661
00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:16.840
You said it was your...

662
00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.140
You can't go to Gap for that.

663
00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:19.240
No, you can't.

664
00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:24.060
You said it was the happiest wardrobe you had ever on a show.

665
00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:30.440
When I realized I would be wearing the same thing for most of the show, that I wasn't changing.

666
00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.480
I didn't have multiple fittings.

667
00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:32.900
Yep.

668
00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:34.460
I was so delighted.

669
00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:35.960
And it was like pajamas.

670
00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:38.260
It was like nice and cozy.

671
00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:39.160
It was.

672
00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:40.120
Didn't hug anywhere.

673
00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:41.920
Yeah, that would be a dream.

674
00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:43.240
It was the beSt.

675
00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:44.680
You don't have to look at a ton of clothes.

676
00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:49.580
But that does explain why you didn't go shopping for a prison jumpsuit.

677
00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:50.240
Correct.

678
00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:50.980
Yeah.

679
00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:52.440
They might not do that now.

680
00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:53.140
It's been a while.

681
00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:55.200
It was 2009 when I last worked in England.

682
00:29:56.620 --> 00:30:00.680
So, well, no, it wasn't 2009, but it was 2009 when I last did a British job.

683
00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:03.720
I did Wonder Woman in England in 2016 or something.

684
00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.880
But again, that was period wardrobe.

685
00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:07.900
So you weren't...

686
00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.340
And they were all made by hand.

687
00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:11.660
And so you weren't going shopping for it.

688
00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:12.040
Yeah.

689
00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:13.900
Oh, so maybe period wardrobe.

690
00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:14.460
Yeah.

691
00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:14.940
Yeah.

692
00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:15.340
Oh, yeah.

693
00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:16.400
Period wardrobe, I wouldn't.

694
00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:17.340
But regular wardrobe.

695
00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:19.960
I've never not been out to shop for it.

696
00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:20.360
Yeah.

697
00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:21.040
Wow.

698
00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:22.000
Side tangent.

699
00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:22.560
Yeah.

700
00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:25.400
Would you come back sometime and talk about Wonder Woman with us?

701
00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:27.840
Because I'm also a huge Wonder Woman fan.

702
00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:28.220
Yes.

703
00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:31.540
I love you in it and would love to have that conversation as well.

704
00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:32.980
In my little curly ginger wig.

705
00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:33.280
Yeah.

706
00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:33.520
Yes.

707
00:30:33.520 --> 00:30:34.800
I love that look.

708
00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.140
I love that look.

709
00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:37.320
Of course.

710
00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:38.060
Yay.

711
00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.380
Put that on our list, Cassie.

712
00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:45.440
Well, in this scene at Front Reception, David Brent is telling Don about sort of his drunken

713
00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:47.280
night out and he's going on and on.

714
00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:51.280
And, you know, we talked about the timing of how things are said.

715
00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:55.800
But for me, it was also about things that were just left out and not said.

716
00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:56.080
Right.

717
00:30:56.080 --> 00:31:02.040
And one of my favorite quotes from this whole episode is David Brent telling you what professionalism

718
00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:04.060
is, telling Don that.

719
00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:05.320
And I think we should hear it.

720
00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:06.920
Because I'm a professional.

721
00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:11.980
And professionalism is, and that is what I want.

722
00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:12.480
Okay?

723
00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:13.020
That's all.

724
00:31:14.980 --> 00:31:15.660
That's it.

725
00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:18.140
And professionalism is.

726
00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:20.240
And he has no idea what it is.

727
00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:20.620
Yeah.

728
00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:21.780
And that is what I want.

729
00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:26.220
Was that the scene or was that episode where he says every guy is woken up at the crack

730
00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:26.620
of dawn?

731
00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:27.500
Crack of dawn.

732
00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:27.800
Yes.

733
00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:28.180
Yes.

734
00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.060
And you're like, what?

735
00:31:29.340 --> 00:31:32.280
Because I, that's the thing that's quoted to me the moSt.

736
00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:32.880
Oh, no.

737
00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:33.320
Really?

738
00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:33.820
Like a dawn.

739
00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:37.920
You must get sentences that you've said or something that's quoted to you.

740
00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:38.360
Oh, yeah.

741
00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:39.120
Yes.

742
00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:39.580
Yeah.

743
00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:43.540
I think we had, but we might have cut it.

744
00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:48.980
Our version of that line, which I think might have gotten cut by standards and practices,

745
00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:53.580
I'm trying to think, is every man has sprayed on Pam.

746
00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:54.360
Oh, yeah.

747
00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:55.080
That was cut.

748
00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:56.360
Yeah, that was cut.

749
00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:59.780
Pam was like, you know, a spray that you put in your pan.

750
00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:00.520
Of course.

751
00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:01.540
Before you cook.

752
00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:01.980
Yeah.

753
00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:03.180
Yes.

754
00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:04.140
I'd forgotten that.

755
00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:04.500
Yeah.

756
00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:07.500
There was this line sprayed on Pam.

757
00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:08.000
Yeah.

758
00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:09.920
Oh, those standards and practices.

759
00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:11.760
That didn't get past them for sure.

760
00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:16.280
You could have said it would have to be bleeped or something.

761
00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:17.520
All right.

762
00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:20.080
So, next up, we're going to meet some more people in the office.

763
00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:23.360
We're going to meet Gareth Keenan, who was played by Mackenzie Crook.

764
00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:31.440
And also, we're going to meet Tim Canterbury, played by Martin Freeman, who I read originally auditioned for Gareth,

765
00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:36.820
which is so funny because John Krasinski originally auditioned for Dwight.

766
00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:38.540
Oh, yeah.

767
00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:39.040
